["I need to make 24 using 10, 1, 2, 6 with each number used once.\n\nLet me try different combinations.\n\n10 + 1 + 2 + 6 = 19, no.\n10 * 1 * 2 + 6 = 26, no.\n10 * 2 + 6 - 1 = 25, no.\n10 * 2 - 6 + 1 = 15, no.\n10 + 2 * 6 + 1 = 23, no.\n10 + 2 * 6 - 1 = 21, no.\n10 * 1 + 2 * 6 = 22, no.\n(10 - 6) * (2 + 1) = 12, no.\n(10 - 6) * 2 * 1 = 8, no.\n(10 + 2) * (6 - 1) = 60, no.\n(10 - 2) * (6 - 1) = 40, no.\n(10 - 2) * 6 * 1 = 48, no.\n(10 - 2) * 6 / 1 = 48, no.\n(10 - 2) * 6 - 1 = 47, no.\n(10 + 2) * 6 * 1 = 72, no.\n(10 + 2) / 6 * 1 = 2, no.\n(10 + 2) * 6 / 1 = 72, no.\n10 * 6 / 2 - 1 = 29, no.\n10 * 6 / 2 + 1 = 31, no.\n10 * 6 / (2 + 1) = 20, no.\n10 * 6 / (2 - 1) = 60, no.\n10 * 6 - 2 * 1 = 58, no.\n10 * 6 - 2 - 1 = 57, no.\n10 * 6 / 2 * 1 = 30, no.\n(10 - 1) * 2 + 6 = 24! Let me check: 10 - 1 = 9, 9 * 2 = 18, 18 + 6 = 24. Yes!\n\nSo (10 - 1) * 2 + 6 = 24.\n\nLet me verify: 10 - 1 = 9, 9 * 2 = 18, 18 + 6 = 24. Correct!\n\nUses 10, 1, 2, 6 each once. \n\nAnother one: (10 - 1) * 2 + 6 = 24.\n"]